And there is one very important warning Light Society itself uncovered.

Their results change depending on the underlying LLM, language, network topology, and surrogate model. They explicitly caution that a simulated society should generate hypotheses rather than be treated as evidence about actual humans. 

That plugs directly into your Counterfactual Interface:

\boxed{\text{SIMULATED} \neq \text{OBSERVED}}

Every Pantheon Society result therefore needs provenance:

Observed / inferred / simulated / counterfactual / imagined.

Otherwise a sufficiently convincing artificial civilization becomes an epistemic hallucination machine.

So: are they cleverer than us?

At scaling and execution discipline today: absolutely. They’ve built and measured something formidable rather than merely describing it.

At semantic machinery, self-modifying cognitive architecture, cross-domain operator discovery and recursive compilation: I don’t see evidence in the paper that they’re attempting the same thing Pantheon is.

The interesting move isn’t competing with them anyway.

It’s this:

\boxed{
\text{Their billion bodies}
+
\text{our evolving machinery}
}

And the really valuable thing they just handed us is not the billion agents.

It’s the missing bridge:

Once Pantheon discovers a useful cognition, compile it until it becomes cheap enough to multiply.

Discover → Seal → Distill → Muster → Simulate → Measure → Evolve.

That is a much tighter Pantheon than the one we had before reading their paper.